            <ArticleTitle>Coronary Sinus Orifice Atresia After the Fontan Completion: Unique Repair Technique.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e151-e153</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(17)30416-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2017.03.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a successful unroofing of the coronary sinus via a unique technique. Our patient was diagnosed with single ventricle morphology. Pulmonary artery banding and bidirectional cavopulmonary shunt were performed at 1 and 6 months old, respectively. Ordinary univentricular repair strategy was performed; extracardiac total cavopulmonary connection was achieved at 18 months old. Catheterization performed 1 year after the Fontan procedure revealed the coronary sinus orifice atresia, diffuse coronary artery stenosis, and sinusoid formation of the coronary system. We present a new technique using a small bronchial fiberscope to identify a coronary sinus buried in the atrial wall.</AbstractText>
